WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.618
A bowl. A ball.

00:00:02.697 --> 00:00:06.392
Watch it step down on my count.
One. Two, three.

00:00:06.551 --> 00:00:11.210
I wrote the words and drew the pictures,
and each picture waits for its word.

00:00:12.214 --> 00:00:16.489
You just watched DeckTalk.
You write a script and simple slides.

00:00:16.881 --> 00:00:21.156
It reads your script aloud, and you
pick the word each picture waits for.

00:00:21.525 --> 00:00:26.393
Change the script, and the video follows.
Here is a lesson I made with it.

00:00:27.234 --> 00:00:31.021
How does AI learn?
From examples, not rules people write.

00:00:31.135 --> 00:00:33.912
Learning from examples
is called machine learning.

00:00:33.945 --> 00:00:36.676
An AI model is like a box of knobs.

00:00:36.731 --> 00:00:41.076
It guesses, and a meter shows its error,
how wrong the guess was.

00:00:41.468 --> 00:00:43.224
Learning nudges the knobs.

00:00:43.593 --> 00:00:47.276
It guesses again, and now it's right,
with a lower error.

00:00:47.621 --> 00:00:51.699
Then a new example,
and a smaller nudge fixes it.

00:00:51.987 --> 00:00:55.194
Example after example,
the model gets better.

00:00:56.201 --> 00:00:58.352
But which way should each knob turn?

00:00:58.477 --> 00:01:01.275
In a big model,
testing knobs one at a time

00:01:01.275 --> 00:01:03.936
would take billions
of tries for every step.

00:01:04.003 --> 00:01:08.069
Instead, one round of math gives
a direction for every knob at once.

00:01:08.926 --> 00:01:12.434
Picture two knobs.
The floor is every way to set them.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:16.231
The height is the error.
For this lesson it is a bowl.

00:01:18.086 --> 00:01:20.793
The ball is where the knobs
are set right now.

00:01:21.104 --> 00:01:24.776
It can't see the whole bowl,
only the slope where it stands.

00:01:24.971 --> 00:01:30.685
So it steps downhill, again and again, and
the steps shrink as the ground flattens.

00:01:30.973 --> 00:01:35.538
That is gradient descent.
It's what the ball did on my count.

00:01:36.476 --> 00:01:39.764
One setting makes every
step longer or shorter.

00:01:40.064 --> 00:01:45.488
Too long, and it bounces past the bottom.
Too short, and it is very slow.

00:01:46.623 --> 00:01:49.772
Zoom out, and the ground
can have many valleys.

00:01:50.187 --> 00:01:53.336
The ball finds a low one,
not always the lowest.

00:01:55.098 --> 00:01:58.212
Today's AI is trained
with a version of this.

00:01:58.593 --> 00:02:02.067
Inside, the knobs are grids of numbers,
called tensors.

00:02:02.912 --> 00:02:08.870
Chips like GPUs and TPUs multiply grids,
row times column, all at once.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.097
For a big model, the chips do

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:14.849
billions of billions of small
calculations for every step.

00:02:15.393 --> 00:02:18.472
The large language models
behind AI chat apps

00:02:18.597 --> 00:02:21.119
split the work across thousands of chips.

00:02:22.269 --> 00:02:25.046
That's the lesson.
Now let's step out of it.

00:02:25.346 --> 00:02:31.931
Everything you just watched is one video,
in five parts, made from one script.

00:02:32.033 --> 00:02:33.475
Remember how it started?

00:02:35.817 --> 00:02:39.512
Watch it step down on my count.
One. Two, three.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.749
Say I want one more step.

00:02:42.037 --> 00:02:46.765
I add one word, four,
and tell a new box to wait for it.

00:02:47.354 --> 00:02:51.629
I press build.
Only part one is read aloud again.

00:02:52.056 --> 00:02:55.983
The rest keep their voice.
Here is the new start.

00:02:56.945 --> 00:03:01.278
Watch it step down on my count.
One. Two, three, four.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.907
One more count, one more box.

00:03:03.907 --> 00:03:08.901
Every picture in the start moved to stay
on its word, and each one is checked.

00:03:09.131 --> 00:03:10.817
Now let's step back in.

00:03:11.653 --> 00:03:14.163
Update your video the way
you update a doc.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:18.517
DeckTalk. Open source,
narrated presentations, cut to the word.

00:03:18.677 --> 00:03:21.002
Make your own at decktalk dot app.
